215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Data-driven solutions offer a promising approach to addressing employability and skills mismatch by leveraging technologies such as data-driven ontologies, skills matching, and skills intelligence. These solutions are designed to tackle the challenges of skills classification, improve job matching, and bridge the gap between education and the labour market. By integrating expert-created classifications with data-driven ontologies, these solutions enable real-time tracking of labour market demand and identifying emerging skills trends. Moreover, annotating educational offerings with taught skills facilitates better measurement of skills supply and estimating the workforce's skill distribution. By aligning job seekers' skills with employers' needs, these data-driven solutions can enhance employability and effectively bridge the skills gap. However, widespread adoption and cooperation of national institutions are still limited, highlighting the need for increased funding and research support to advance the implementation of data-driven solutions in this field. \(Robert Rentzsch 1, 2020\)  )
/E (Data-driven solutions offer a promising approach to addressing employability and skills mismatch by leveraging technologies such as data-driven ontologies, skills matching, and skills intelligence. These solutions are designed to tackle the challenges of skills classification, improve job matching, and bridge the gap between education and the labour market. By integrating expert-created classifications with data-driven ontologies, these solutions enable real-time tracking of labour market demand and identifying emerging skills trends. Moreover, annotating educational offerings with taught skills facilitates better measurement of skills supply and estimating the workforce's skill distribution. By aligning job seekers' skills with employers' needs, these data-driven solutions can enhance employability and effectively bridge the skills gap. However, widespread adoption and cooperation of national institutions are still limited, highlighting the need for increased funding and research support to advance the implementation of data-driven solutions in this field. \(Robert Rentzsch 1, 2020\)  )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Addressing employability and skills mismatch necessitates a multifaceted approach that fosters collaboration among educational institutions, employers, policymakers, and individuals. This challenge affects a diverse range of stakeholders within the employment ecosystem, including policymakers, employers, students and workers. Skills mismatch is a recurring issue in vocational education and skills development, prompting numerous policy initiatives aimed at mitigating this challenge)
/E (Addressing employability and skills mismatch necessitates a multifaceted approach that fosters collaboration among educational institutions, employers, policymakers, and individuals. This challenge affects a diverse range of stakeholders within the employment ecosystem, including policymakers, employers, students and workers. Skills mismatch is a recurring issue in vocational education and skills development, prompting numerous policy initiatives aimed at mitigating this challenge)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
